Current test:
```ts
it("requires a Worker release for production Worker config changes", async () => {
	const fixture = await createFixture();
	await writeFixtureFile(fixture.root, "cloudflare.config.ts", 'export const workerName = "changed";\n');
	await writeChangeset(fixture.root, []);

	await expect(
		runCheck(fixture, {
			changedFiles: ["cloudflare.config.ts"],
			changesetDiffLines: ["A\t.changeset/new.md"],
		}),
	).rejects.toThrow("cloudflare.config.ts -> @hevy-mcp/worker");
});
```

The thrown error is the EMPTY-changeset error:
"Empty Changesets cannot accompany release-triggering changes:\n- .changeset/new.md\nRelease-triggering changes require non-empty bumps:\n- cloudflare.config.ts -> @hevy-mcp/worker"

The mapping substring "cloudflare.config.ts -> @hevy-mcp/worker" appears in BOTH the "need a changeset" error AND the empty-changeset error — so the current assertion could pass for the wrong reason. Valid comment. Fix per its suggestion — "using the existing runCheck behavior and symbols": the catch pattern like the coupling test, asserting BOTH the empty-changeset reason AND the worker mapping:

```ts
const failure = await runCheck(fixture, {
	changedFiles: ["cloudflare.config.ts"],
	changesetDiffLines: ["A\t.changeset/new.md"],
